WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.987
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.640
- Well, welcome to a special
episode of the Addy Hour.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.600
Today I'm honored to be able to host

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.863
two scientific directors

00:00:11.863 --> 00:00:14.070
from the National Institutes of Health,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.330
for a conversation we're
really gonna be focusing

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.910
on physical and mental wellness

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.600
and also talking about racial disparities

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.660
specifically within the context
of our scientific research

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.230
and in the context of
our scientific workforce,

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.970
also with an emphasis on
our scientific trainees.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.050
So I'm honored to
welcome two guests today.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:35.090
The first of which is Dr. Amy Newman.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.400
Dr. Newman is the scientific director

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.770
at the National Institute on Drug Abuse,

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.230
a role that she has served in since 2020.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.063
And prior to that,

00:00:44.063 --> 00:00:46.380
she was actually the
acting scientific director

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.020
for two years.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.710
Dr. Newman received her
undergraduate degree in chemistry

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:52.530
at Mary Washington College

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.130
and that also went on after that

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.670
to a PhD in medicinal chemistry

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.978
from the Medical College of Virginia

00:00:57.978 --> 00:01:00.320
and Virginia Commonwealth University,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.960
then went on to post-doctoral
training at NIDDK.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.617
Dr. Newman is someone who has
been prolific in the field

00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:12.060
and has contributed immensely
to our knowledge in this area.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.420
As it was stated during
the NIH press release

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.610
when she took this position,

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.890
she has performed work

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.190
focusing on design,
synthesis and evaluation

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.170
of central nervous system acting agents

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.980
as potential therapeutics
for substance use disorders.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.505
She has published over 300 articles,

00:01:27.505 --> 00:01:31.510
is also an inventor on
multiple NIH patents

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.380
And one of the things I really
appreciate about Dr. Newman

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.210
in addition to her outstanding science

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.300
is her emphasis on
training and mentorship.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.060
So in 2019, she received a
Kirschstein Mentoring Award

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.420
from the office of the director of the NIH

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.220
and in 2018 was recognized

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.070
as a remarkable woman
in medicinal chemistry

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.840
from the American Chemical Society.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.190
So someone who has had a lot
of impact both scientifically

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.810
and in terms of people's
training and development

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:00.643
over the years.

00:02:00.643 --> 00:02:02.860
So I'm honored to be able
to welcome Dr. Newman

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.621
to the Addy Hour podcast.

00:02:04.621 --> 00:02:06.290
- Thank you so much, Nii.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.720
- Oh, thanks so much for being here.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.860
- Our second guest, which
I'm pleased to introduce

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.630
is Dr. Luigi Ferrucci,

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.430
who is the scientific director

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.721
at the National Institute on Aging.

00:02:16.721 --> 00:02:19.930
Dr. Ferrucci is a geriatrician
and an epidemiologist

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.930
who conducts research
on the causal pathways

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.630
leading to progressive
physical and cognitive decline

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.340
in older persons.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.410
He received a lot of
his training at Florence

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.760
at the University of Florence in Italy

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.720
where he also received his PhD

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.540
and has board certification in geriatrics.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.690
He served on the faculty
at that university

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:41.690
for quite a while,

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.500
an associate professor of biology,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.700
human physiology, and statistics

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.150
at the University of Florence.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.770
And also between 1985 and 2002,

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.540
was the chief of geriatric rehabilitation

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.370
at the Department of Geriatric Medicine

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:57.930
and director of the Laboratory
of Clinical Epidemiology

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.420
at the Italian National
Institute of Aging.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.870
So Luigi, as someone who
has performed research

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.230
across several different continents,

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:09.560
a lot of work at both
in the US and in Europe,

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.850
a lot of longitudinal studies

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.750
specifically looking at
risk factors for mobility,

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.760
disability in older persons.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:16.960
In September of 2002,

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.480
he became the chief of a
longitudinal studies section

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.410
at the National Institute on Aging

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:22.470
and the director

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.460
of the Baltimore
Longitudinal Study on Aging.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.350
And then in 2001, became the
scientific director at NIA.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:32.660
So someone who has a longstanding
history in the field,

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.200
has made several contributions,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.960
and has also served in several
different leadership roles.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.980
So again, honored to be able
to welcome Dr. Luigi Ferrucci

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.520
to the Addy Hour podcast as well.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:43.820
- Thank you very much.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.243
I'm looking forward to this conversation.

00:03:46.243 --> 00:03:47.407
- Yes, I am as well.

00:03:47.407 --> 00:03:49.780
And I feel like this
is a two for one deal.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:51.510
I know the two of you interact quite a bit

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:52.343
and I'm honored to be able

00:03:52.343 --> 00:03:54.200
to host both of you simultaneously

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.870
and to hear some
complimentary perspectives

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.590
that you all have in your
roles as scientific directors

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.950
and in your roles in mentoring
and in leadership as well.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.100
So as our listeners know,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.760
I always like to start out just
checking in with our guests

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.610
to see how they're doing.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:10.780
And before we get there, though,

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.653
I did want to put this in
context a little bit as well,

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:15.660
because I think there are
some things as scientists

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.870
that we all share in common,

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:19.690
especially with everything that's gone on

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.100
over the last 18 months,

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.500
I think a lot of us as scientists

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.770
have become defacto
experts for our friends

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.420
and our colleagues and families,

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.990
whether we feel like we
fit that role well or not.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.190
But just with the way

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:35.510
that science has been talked
about publicly recently,

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.410
obviously with COVID and
also with the last election,

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:42.390
I feel like science has been
elevated in the conversation,

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.350
some for good, some for not so good,

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.930
but it's given us an opportunity
in a lot of ways as well.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.150
But I know for me personally,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.217
and others I've talked to,

00:04:50.217 --> 00:04:53.660
we've sometimes become defacto
infectious disease experts

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.770
or vaccine experts or
immunologists or epidemiologists.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:58.949
Of course, Luigi, in your
case, that is appropriate

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:01.530
in terms of the epidemiology.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.390
But I did want to also just talk

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.040
about the ways that we are also all

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.460
going through this as well
just like everybody else,

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.740
and not always an easy journey.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.700
So if we could just start
talking a little bit

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:15.190
about how you've personally
experienced the last 18 months

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:18.440
and how that has also played
out in your professional role

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.380
as a scientist working
with scientific trainees.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.480
So Dr. Newman, if we could start with you.

00:05:24.455 --> 00:05:25.288
- Thank you, Nii.

00:05:25.288 --> 00:05:26.830
It has been a roller coaster,

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.280
and I think that I
probably have many people

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.510
on that roller coaster with me.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.170
As you noted, I became the
actual scientific director

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.290
just in November.

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.870
I was the acting scientific
director before that.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.990
So within this 18 months,

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.210
I was also applying for the job

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.180
that I was in and hope to remain in

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.440
experiencing the many changes

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.260
that we had to quickly make
to have people, first of all,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.410
work from home and figure
out how to do that,

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:55.810
and then slowly get back,

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:00.810
but even now being distanced
and masked and so forth,

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.690
it has been quite an adjustment.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:05.940
And I think that where
I get the most thrill

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.510
is that we're doing it.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:11.600
And despite the fact
that it's not been easy

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:14.460
and there's certainly
been trauma along the way,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.890
we have figured it out for the most part

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.770
at just about every level,

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:20.590
not to say that we don't want to get back,

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:21.670
we all want to get back,

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:25.467
we want to be together and
do the work that we do best

00:06:25.467 --> 00:06:26.760
and that we love most,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.120
but I think that I'm really delighted

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.180
with the resilience that I've seen,

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.290
and for the most part, the flexibility

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.250
that we've had to have at every level

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.410
in order to kind of keep
on doing what we're doing,

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:39.580
because we can't stop.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.490
We're scientists and
not only do we love it,

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:42.400
but I think,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.120
you know, I really think
the work that we do

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.650
is so important to continue to do

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:47.740
in the best way that we can.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.015
- Yeah, very well said

00:06:49.015 --> 00:06:50.270
and there's a lot of hope there as well.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:52.620
I like the full picture that you gave,

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:53.620
not that it's been easy

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:55.010
or that there haven't been
challenges along the way,

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:56.940
but that things are still moving forward.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.775
I think that's really important

00:06:58.775 --> 00:07:00.770
and I appreciate you
bringing that out as well.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:02.117
What about you, Luigi?

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:07.850
- Certainly, I agree with
most of what Amy said.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.607
It's been challenging.

00:07:09.607 --> 00:07:12.000
I think the most challenging part to me

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:17.850
has been losing that direct connection,

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:23.220
that unspoken language that
connects people and scientists

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.120
and being able and make an effort

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.330
to continuously connect with them

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:33.330
so that you become the
thread that (mumbles)

00:07:33.575 --> 00:07:34.623
the spirit of it.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:39.140
And I have to say, also what Amy say,

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:43.010
I'm amazed how people have
responded to this challenge,

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:47.970
how the scientific creativity
did not get run down.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.550
People found any possible way

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:55.020
to maintain attention
and focus on their work.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:56.610
They could not collect data.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:57.970
They used the data they had

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:01.280
to analyze them and writing their paper.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:06.280
They kept having their
lab meeting virtually.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:11.700
I have to say that people
have been marvelous

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:13.710
to me how they,

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.350
of course there's been weaknesses,

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.023
there have been, some of our trainees,

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:21.543
and then not only the trainees,

00:08:21.543 --> 00:08:25.970
have been really subject to great stress.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:30.117
And for me, it's been an attempt

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:35.400
to support them in many different ways.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:36.550
And at the same time,

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:39.100
I was dealing with my personal situation

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:43.610
because my family is in
Italy so we couldn't travel.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:48.610
So I stay 18 months
without visiting my family.

00:08:48.972 --> 00:08:50.880
And so there was the
stress of not traveling,

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.361
the stress of having to maintain (mumbles)

00:08:55.361 --> 00:08:58.172
so that at the end of
this period we will have

00:08:58.172 --> 00:09:01.370
an NIA and NIDA are still alive

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:04.343
and can produce that
important work that they do.

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:06.240
It's a mixed bag.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:07.260
I learn a lot.

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:10.770
I think that if we get out from here,

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.220
the grief of COVID-19 release,

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.790
we'll do things differently

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.543
because we have learned something.

00:09:19.142 --> 00:09:20.940
- Yeah, that's very well said as well.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.500
There's so many important
points that you both brought up,

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.010
just the realities of the whole thing.

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.290
And I like that you talked
about the personal aspect

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:29.260
for you as well 'cause
you're still leading,

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:30.450
even though you yourself

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:33.173
haven't seen your family in 18 months.

00:09:34.092 --> 00:09:34.925
And then Amy, as you mentioned,

00:09:34.925 --> 00:09:37.610
just the resilience that's been
there as well on both sides,

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.860
even while there are
challenges along the way.

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:41.960
Several different points I
wanted to actually follow up on,

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:45.110
but one, Luigi, you mentioned it

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:48.060
and hinted at the expectation in a sense

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:50.825
for NIA and for NIDA.

00:09:50.825 --> 00:09:52.080
There's an expectation that
the work will continue.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.170
How have you seen that impact

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:56.750
yourselves and those around you?

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:58.480
Has that been good pressure?

00:09:58.480 --> 00:09:59.750
Has it been challenging pressure?

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.700
Especially as you said, some
have thrived under this,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.200
but some have also had
challenges with the stress.

00:10:06.321 --> 00:10:08.040
Anything that you've noticed
that's come out with that?

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.640
- I think globally, we
are doing fantastic.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:16.640
I think that there has been
an episode of difficulty,

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:20.683
but even those that when under stress,

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:26.240
adequately supported, they
were able to come out positive.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.480
I've seen that the people
were highly receptive

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:33.050
to discussion, to communication.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:34.033
And so in general,

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:38.100
you know, certainly everything went

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.710
as perfect as we wanted it to.

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:43.339
And you know, one of
the things that you said

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:45.080
at the beginning I think
was very important.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:46.930
You know, at some point everybody

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:51.560
became expert in epidemiology
of viral disease.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.046
You know, everybody was a virologist,

00:10:54.046 --> 00:10:57.350
everybody was trying to write on COVID-19.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:00.530
Initially, I was kind of upset at that,

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:01.363
you know, this is the science,

00:11:01.363 --> 00:11:03.840
you can't really improvise yourself to be,

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.800
but then I realized that to some extent

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.713
this was a coping strategy.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:12.710
Instead of being only the receiver

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:15.880
of the negative effect of COVID-19,

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:20.140
being a protagonist and
contribute in some way another

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:21.960
with your scientific expertise

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:23.900
the solution of the problem.

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.790
And so I realized that all these

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.850
trying to elaborate the new ideas,

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:32.380
especially in the field of aging

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:36.243
where COVID-19's really
been hitting the weaker.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:38.590
Older people are certainly

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:41.860
one of the weakest
component of our population,

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:44.533
it was really extraordinarily positive.

00:11:45.798 --> 00:11:46.834
So in the end,

00:11:46.834 --> 00:11:49.570
I think that my view is very positive.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.150
As I said, we'll come out from this

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:54.720
a little bit better than we were before,

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:55.720
that's what I think.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:11:59.310
- I think from our side,

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:04.160
there's two groups that
really suffered a lot.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:07.700
And one were those who
have small children at home

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:09.020
or school age children at home.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:13.560
And this does hit not only our
post-docs and junior faculty,

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.780
but also many of our administrators

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:19.010
who we completely depend
on to keep us going forward

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:20.730
and having to figure out

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:24.520
how to teach your children from home

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:26.470
and get your kindergardener on a laptop

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:27.840
and those kinds of things

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.790
I think was extremely
stressful for that group.

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:32.810
And I think some of them really felt

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.770
like they were not able to move forward

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:37.150
in the job that they wanted to do

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:39.220
because they had this whole new job

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:41.470
that was extremely new to them

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:43.650
and not something they
were necessarily good at

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:46.570
and they had to do it,
there was no other choice.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.460
I think that was really tough.

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:50.840
The other group,

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:52.450
our clinical researchers,

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:56.890
and we work with substance use disorders

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:59.350
and that has skyrocketed during COVID.

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:03.810
I mean that is just a tremendous
number of overdose deaths,

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:05.970
much higher than it has
been in several years

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:08.640
and can't even do our patient studies,

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.550
we can't do our clinical work right now

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.180
to the degree that we had before.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.440
We've just started to open up

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:14.880
some clinical studies this summer,

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.450
but it's at a much slower capacity.

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:20.960
And so I think our clinical
researchers feel frustrated

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:22.500
because this is what they do

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:25.510
and that's the population
that they want to serve.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:27.110
It's really suffered.

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:30.260
And they're kind of, if anything slow,

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:31.730
but almost stopped in their tracks

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:33.053
for the last 18 months.

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:35.110
- What have been some of the ways

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:38.460
that you all have tried to
address that in the meantime,

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:40.080
just being in that place of,

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:42.810
it sounds like a mixture
of one, feeling stuck,

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:45.130
and two, not being able to help the people

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:46.900
that you feel like you're
called to serve as well.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:48.493
So how do you address that?

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:54.870
- Well, I think that
the most difficult part

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:57.720
is that when you are in the building,

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.020
when you meet people in the corridor,

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:03.500
when you get coffee with them,

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:08.370
when you have all these
informal meeting with them,

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:10.830
that they are not predisposed,

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:13.920
you keep track of what is happening.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:15.470
You're feeling the sense

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:20.020
of whether there is something
wrong going on in the people,

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:22.030
the gesture, the posture,

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:26.930
the way they say hello
to you at the moment.

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:31.203
And I felt that I was
completely missing that.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:35.489
For example, this is a stupid example,

00:14:35.489 --> 00:14:38.673
but to tell you how
much this was important.

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:42.280
During Christmas time last year,

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:46.770
I had so much longing for the
people that work in my office

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:49.010
of the scientific director,

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:51.223
that I visited each one.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:53.740
I went to their home,

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:55.630
I saw them for one minute,

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:57.063
but we were face-to-face.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:00.800
And I got, I don't think
they got a lot from it,

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:02.770
but I did, I did like,

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:06.573
I felt that that human
connection, what it constitutes,

00:15:07.666 --> 00:15:08.499
and what I'm afraid

00:15:08.499 --> 00:15:11.440
is that while our
productivity will be high,

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:16.440
we established those
informal and heart connection

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.290
is not gonna be automatic,

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:22.340
we will need to work hard to do that.

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:27.340
And I intend to do it
by meeting with people,

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:33.100
creating informal way of creating groups,

00:15:35.679 --> 00:15:39.000
even not based on the scientific,

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:44.000
but Thursday last week
at the end of the day,

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:49.280
we went for a walk, masking, everything,

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:51.210
it was incredible,

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:54.950
30 people just for two months of walking,

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:57.240
but interacting with them

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:00.430
and see how bright

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:03.690
and inventive and creative they are,

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:05.290
I think that gave me a lot.

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:07.090
But I think also they felt

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:10.000
that their scientific director,

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.246
their leader was with them,

00:16:12.246 --> 00:16:15.040
and I want them to feel
that I am with them,

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:18.270
I learn from them and I feel they are

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:19.313
in my same journey.

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:22.682
- That's great, all in
the journey together.

00:16:22.682 --> 00:16:24.870
Amy, anything you were
gonna add there as well?

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:26.920
- I did not do the one-on-one like that,

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:28.280
but one thing that we did

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:31.260
was that we had listening
sessions on Zoom,

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:34.160
but one-on-one with every
single faculty member

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:37.410
and they had just 30 minutes,
but I had two questions.

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:41.160
One is tell me about one
most exciting project

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.170
that you're working on
or you want to work on.

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:46.290
And then tell me the
challenges that you have

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.670
to prevent you from
moving forward as quickly

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.930
or as effectively as you want to.

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:52.350
And they were,

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:53.700
and we did this with all the faculty

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:54.670
and then we did it in groups

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:56.440
with our staff scientists
and staff clinicians

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:58.190
and we even did a group of the post-docs

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:01.770
and I think that it was so super fun.

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:04.870
It was great to see that
enthusiasm despite the situation.

00:17:04.870 --> 00:17:08.150
It was very informative to
hear what the challenges were.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.400
Many of them I knew, but
some of them I didn't

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:11.330
and also I didn't know

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:14.510
how many people found certain
things to be challenging.

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.500
And so that gave me my orders

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:19.870
to move forward and try
to fix those things.

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:23.440
But especially with like my
scientists and staff clinicians,

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:26.130
they said, "I've never had a chance

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:27.570
to talk to my scientific director.

00:17:27.570 --> 00:17:29.770
This is the first time I've ever talked

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:30.860
to my scientific director,

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.147
they ever asked me to
have a conversation."

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:37.030
I got a lot out of that,
but I think they did too.

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:39.650
And the other thing
that I did this summer,

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:40.500
'cause we could do it,

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.070
I have a nice backyard,

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:43.580
we live kind of out in the country

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:45.970
and we lined up big picnic tables

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:48.800
and I invited my whole
group for outdoor lunch

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:52.340
and I made lunch and we were all outside

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:53.870
and it was so fun,

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:55.710
I mean we hung out here for hours

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:58.530
and it was just a great
chance to be together.

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.640
And again, I loved it,

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:02.690
but I think they really loved it too.

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:04.090
And it was just a one-off,

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:05.610
I wish I could do it more often.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:07.230
This is something that
I really like to do.

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:09.648
Luigi and I both love cooking
and we love to entertain

00:18:09.648 --> 00:18:13.030
and it's something that we've
really not been able to do

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:14.300
like we want to,

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:16.130
but those little tiny gestures

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:19.280
seem to have been useful for all of us.

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:20.113
- Yeah, that's great

00:18:20.113 --> 00:18:21.410
and I appreciate you
peeling back the layers

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:23.460
'cause as you were both talking about how

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:27.130
groups are still here, people
are still moving forward,

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:28.060
that was one thing I was
just really wondering,

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:31.064
like the layers underneath
that what you had done.

00:18:31.064 --> 00:18:32.800
And it sounds like it's
been some new creativity

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:33.633
in that sense too.

00:18:33.633 --> 00:18:36.660
And as we were talking before we started,

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:37.760
the session as well,

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:40.600
the things that we don't get
trained on as scientists,

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:43.740
but which I think we're all
becoming much more aware

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:45.360
of the importance of,

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:47.070
you know, just having people over

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:48.170
or having those touch points,

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:50.570
Luigi, having your drive-by's

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:52.610
to just connect with people

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.670
and connect and chat with them

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:57.470
and how important that is for
all of us in so many ways.

00:18:59.281 --> 00:19:00.114
- You know that,

00:19:00.114 --> 00:19:01.830
but you learn more and more

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:06.830
that only place that make
people enthusiastic and happy

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:10.640
becomes productive.

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:15.307
You need to make them
wanting to come to work.

00:19:15.307 --> 00:19:18.700
You need to make them being thinking

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:20.440
that the best thing they can do

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:22.630
is to interact with the group.

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:25.260
And if you're able to do that,

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:28.850
that is an incredibly powerful force.

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:32.210
You don't really have to
teach them how to pipette.

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:35.750
You need to tell them if they
come here and they pipette,

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:40.060
they will make a discovery and
they will be extremely happy

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:41.540
because of that discovery.

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:44.493
I think that that is
the most important thing

00:19:44.493 --> 00:19:46.633
that we need to do as leaders.

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:52.910
And then they will grow
professionally by themselves.

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:55.380
You know somebody told me once

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:58.690
that when you have trainees,

00:19:58.690 --> 00:20:02.285
you don't have to take
their hands continuously.

00:20:02.285 --> 00:20:06.133
You are like a flight control person

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:12.080
that follow them and you
tell them where to go,

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.150
but they actually know where to go

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:18.050
and you only intervene
when there is bad weather

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:22.180
or when they for some reason
are losing their direction

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:24.360
and then you have to go there,

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:26.060
put them in the right direction

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:29.190
and then allow them to fly by themself.

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:33.320
And I find that creating
a good environment

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:35.670
and a safety net where they know

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:38.540
if they get in trouble,
there's somebody there

00:20:39.386 --> 00:20:40.219
that will help them,

00:20:40.219 --> 00:20:43.900
it's really the best way
to handle our trainees.

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:46.290
- Yeah, it sounds like
it's building a community

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:48.410
and making sure that it's
sincere and authentic

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:50.980
and that people know that
you're there to be supportive,

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:52.380
which is so important.

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:54.050
Amy, would you say those
are similar principles

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:56.790
that you try to apply as well?

00:20:56.790 --> 00:21:01.201
- It reminded me of a quote
from your previous podcast,

00:21:01.201 --> 00:21:05.197
Dr. Ressler and Jones Marlin,
when they said in quotes,

00:21:05.197 --> 00:21:07.340
"Cohesion can buffer trauma."

00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:08.173
And I love that.

00:21:08.173 --> 00:21:10.770
I wrote that down because I thought, yes,

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:14.430
if we have that community where
we have these relationships,

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:15.810
which I think are so important,

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:19.270
and like Luigi, I want
people to love their science,

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.107
I want them to be able to pipette,

00:21:21.107 --> 00:21:23.190
or in my case, I want them
to be able to run a reaction

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:25.897
and not blow themselves
up, it's really important.

00:21:27.714 --> 00:21:30.030
But having said that,
what I always say to them

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:32.720
is it's as important

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:34.600
to really work on your
communication skills

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:36.200
and your development of relationships

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:37.550
'cause as chemists,

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:39.130
we can make compounds all day long,

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:40.790
but if I don't have neuroscientists

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:42.010
and behavioral pharmacologists

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:45.114
and computational biophysicist
and all those people

00:21:45.114 --> 00:21:49.500
to help me put those
molecules into perspective

00:21:49.500 --> 00:21:52.040
and to help me know what tools are needed

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:54.530
and then give me the opportunity to say,

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:57.870
wow, we just made something
that nobody else has ever made

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.080
and we just locked the biological secret

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:04.627
that would have continued to
be elusive without that tool.

00:22:04.627 --> 00:22:08.850
And that's the thing I think
is so much fun about what we do

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:11.020
and I hope is the role model

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:12.720
that we play as scientific directors

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:15.390
for all those who work with us.

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:16.970
- Yeah so important, so well said,

00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:19.140
just the importance of
that diverse community

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:21.980
in terms of our science and
our teams and things like that.

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:23.250
And of course that brings up a topic

00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:24.890
that I wanted to kind of touch on as well

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:27.540
'cause clearly the scientific
diversity is so important,

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:30.050
but we've also been just
trying to walk through

00:22:30.050 --> 00:22:32.090
all the racial diversity issues,

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:33.300
the racial reckoning

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:35.470
that many of us are trying to walk through

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:37.810
and hoping that it's a
true racial reckoning,

00:22:37.810 --> 00:22:40.580
but then also seeing ups
and downs along the way

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:41.690
and all the racial disparities.

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:42.760
I know both of your institutes

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:45.530
have aspects of health
disparities research as well,

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:46.530
but I'm just curious

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:48.410
as we've been talking along
the lines of the COVID,

00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:51.010
like how has those components

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:53.410
impacted you all on a day-to-day basis

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:54.840
in your roles as directors

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.320
and in your interactions with your teams?

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:00.930
- So I guess one of the things
that's come out of it for us

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:05.060
is again, this kind of grassroots growing

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:07.930
of acknowledgement of
the importance of that

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:09.690
and not only do we have

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:12.890
kind of a faculty-led diversity committee,

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:15.610
but we have a trainee-led, started,

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:17.420
and it's called the IDEA Committee,

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:19.903
inclusion, diversity, equity,

00:23:19.903 --> 00:23:23.780
and they're educating us.

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:26.530
So every month, they send out an email

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:28.080
where they're they're explaining

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:30.080
some aspect of racial diversity

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:33.600
or like during black history
month or different things.

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:34.730
They're educating us,

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:37.150
they're bringing speakers
into our institution.

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:39.540
What they are doing is
being part of a solution,

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:41.730
which frankly we need.

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:45.400
We need everyone I think
to be part of that solution

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:47.930
and to try to help us improve

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:51.540
and really recognize the
importance of diversity

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:52.390
in that community

00:23:52.390 --> 00:23:55.280
and how it really brings science

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:56.880
to the very best that it can be.

00:23:58.400 --> 00:23:59.560
- That's great to hear.

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:02.095
Luigi, anything you want to add there?

00:24:02.095 --> 00:24:05.030
- I think that what Amy said

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:07.273
is absolutely the right direction.

00:24:08.499 --> 00:24:13.499
I have the feeling that
we need to attract more.

00:24:17.391 --> 00:24:19.010
As an NIH environment,

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:21.270
we have so much to offer

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:25.610
and so we're not doing enough outreach.

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:29.030
We're not showing our cards around,

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:30.930
showing how much being in NIH

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:35.490
could be a very successful environment,

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:37.130
for everybody,

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:39.580
for underrepresented minority,

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:42.463
the disadvantaged young trainees.

00:24:47.277 --> 00:24:49.640
I think that at some
point I did not realize

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:52.210
that I was part of the problem

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:53.700
because yes I thought,

00:24:53.700 --> 00:24:56.110
we need to outreach to minorities,

00:24:56.110 --> 00:24:59.216
diversify our workforce is important,

00:24:59.216 --> 00:25:02.460
that's one of many things
that I needed to do.

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:05.060
And I am glad that at some point

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:08.280
this was transformed into a central theme.

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:11.410
And so we got a lot of information

00:25:11.410 --> 00:25:14.720
and in a way, I realize much more

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:19.720
that this was an incredibly important part

00:25:20.430 --> 00:25:23.850
of what NIH need to transform itself

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:27.783
and become a true scientific enterprise.

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:32.110
- That time, I think that many, many

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:35.210
try to address the problem,

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:40.210
I still feel that we don't
have enough people coming in

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:44.690
when we advertise for our position.

00:25:44.690 --> 00:25:48.930
So what we need to do
is to go and stimulate

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:53.940
the progress of young investigator

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:57.293
that are attracted by coming to NIH

00:25:58.181 --> 00:26:00.343
and realize their scientific dreams.

00:26:01.770 --> 00:26:05.624
I think that we're doing
a little bit of outreach,

00:26:05.624 --> 00:26:07.793
but I think we should do better.

00:26:09.016 --> 00:26:12.290
I'm discussing with some of
the other scientific director,

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:16.690
we should create a movie
about what NIH does,

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:20.540
showcase some example of high success,

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:25.073
show the technology and
the good studies at NIH

00:26:28.096 --> 00:26:30.520
and then go to the different
academic institution.

00:26:30.520 --> 00:26:32.513
If we do not expand that base,

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:35.030
I don't think the solution

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:38.110
is fighting for the few candidates.

00:26:38.110 --> 00:26:38.943
I don't think the solution

00:26:38.943 --> 00:26:43.440
is that Yale and NIH fight
to get that candidate

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:44.560
and offer more.

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:48.030
We need to expand that base
'cause the base is there.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:51.420
So we need to find ways to realize that.

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:54.360
And I think that many, many people now

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:57.100
are really considering

00:26:57.100 --> 00:27:00.120
that's it's an important
part of our mission

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:04.670
and so I am hopeful that
with some up and down,

00:27:04.670 --> 00:27:05.880
as you were explaining,

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:10.880
I think that puts the political
situation where we live

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:12.280
will impact that,

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:14.900
but if we have enough time,

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:18.020
I think we can make a
dent on this problem.

00:27:18.020 --> 00:27:20.440
- And again, that makes me
think about the importance

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:21.850
of the community aspect as well,

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:23.700
both the community and the communication,

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:26.900
because you're talking about
making sure that the community

00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:29.700
is projecting and expanding,

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:31.410
but that through the communication

00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:32.320
of those in the community

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:34.790
to make sure that other
communities are pulled in as well

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:37.195
and I think that's really important

00:27:37.195 --> 00:27:39.400
is what the environment is like.

00:27:39.400 --> 00:27:40.790
And Amy, as you were mentioning,

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:43.640
it's encouraging to hear
about the trainees stepping up

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:46.520
and providing those resources
and all those things as well.

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:48.140
I was gonna ask a follow-up question there

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:49.540
because that even in itself

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:52.130
can sometimes be a
point of tension as well

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:55.100
in terms of where the
onus is with the trainees

00:27:56.536 --> 00:27:57.580
as they are going through their training

00:27:57.580 --> 00:27:58.730
and taking on extra work.

00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:01.920
So how has that whole dynamic played out?

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:03.240
Because we've seen it in a lot of ways,

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:05.356
Black in Neuro is a good example as well,

00:28:05.356 --> 00:28:06.724
where the trainees have really stepped up

00:28:06.724 --> 00:28:07.557
and done a tremendous job,

00:28:07.557 --> 00:28:10.250
but also know that they
need to balance their time

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:11.530
in terms of being trainees

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:14.480
and that they also need the
support of administrators

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:18.140
to actually implement some of the changes

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:19.660
and to be able to incorporate.

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:24.470
So have those conversations
been part of the process?

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:26.720
From your response, I'm
thinking it has been.

00:28:27.576 --> 00:28:28.409
- The thing is that

00:28:28.409 --> 00:28:29.920
what a wonderful time to learn it, right?

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:32.810
So when you're in this
kind of post-baccalaureate

00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:33.643
or post-doctoral time,

00:28:33.643 --> 00:28:34.540
you're learning science

00:28:34.540 --> 00:28:37.150
and how to figure out how
to do research and so forth,

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:40.120
but also that you're not just a scientist.

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:41.556
As Luigi said in the beginning,

00:28:41.556 --> 00:28:43.260
if we're just scientists,

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:44.430
we're not doing our job.

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:46.910
We really need to learn
to do these other things

00:28:46.910 --> 00:28:48.480
and find out what we're good at.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:49.980
Not all of us are good at all things,

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:52.206
that's why the diversity comes into it

00:28:52.206 --> 00:28:53.039
as being so important.

00:28:53.039 --> 00:28:55.050
And so, yes, I think some of our trainees

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:56.810
end up spending a lot of time

00:28:56.810 --> 00:28:59.220
on these committee responsibilities

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:00.840
and not so much time in the laboratory

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:01.673
and then sometimes

00:29:01.673 --> 00:29:03.140
we have to reel them back in a little bit,

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:04.240
but on the other hand,

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:05.930
if that's what they really love

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:08.410
and that's how they really contribute,

00:29:08.410 --> 00:29:10.410
then I think that that's great.

00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:12.130
I mean one thing I was gonna go back to

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:13.810
with regard to the community

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:14.900
and not the scientific community,

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:16.450
but in Baltimore, for example,

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:18.200
people wonder why we're,

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:20.480
the National Institute on
Drug Abuse is in Baltimore

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:21.950
and it's largely

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:23.990
because Baltimore had
the drug abuse problem,

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:25.240
but those that didn't,

00:29:25.240 --> 00:29:27.100
Baltimore has had an opioid crisis

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:29.950
for a lot longer than the
rest of the country has,

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:33.520
and yet we still have
trouble recruiting subjects

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.060
into our studies.

00:29:35.060 --> 00:29:36.600
And I think in part,

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:38.810
there is this they don't
know that we exist,

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:39.980
that we're there,

00:29:39.980 --> 00:29:44.050
in part, there is a distrust
government or mental work,

00:29:44.050 --> 00:29:46.260
and we have to somehow figure it out.

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:47.570
And I don't any of the
answers for all this.

00:29:47.570 --> 00:29:49.330
This is really where I'm trying

00:29:49.330 --> 00:29:51.680
to find better ways to do things

00:29:51.680 --> 00:29:54.530
and trying to encourage
our trainees and others,

00:29:54.530 --> 00:29:55.363
faculty members,

00:29:55.363 --> 00:29:58.360
to try to break down
some of those barriers

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:01.610
because we are not going to
address the health disparities,

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:03.670
particularly of substance use disorder,

00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:04.970
if we can't get subjects

00:30:04.970 --> 00:30:06.730
into our clinical trials and our studies

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:08.610
and understand what
works and what doesn't.

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:10.970
And that's a big challenge

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:14.100
that we are really not doing well,

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:15.290
and I think that that's one

00:30:15.290 --> 00:30:17.480
of our big challenges going forward.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:19.100
- Yeah and it seems like it has so many

00:30:19.100 --> 00:30:21.660
ramifications as well
as you've talked about,

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.480
just from the research itself

00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:25.350
and the community and impact.

00:30:25.350 --> 00:30:27.578
I know Amy within,

00:30:27.578 --> 00:30:28.960
and I'm gonna not remember
exactly the name of it,

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:30.650
but I know there is a big push

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:32.180
to make sure that there is more diversity

00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:33.370
in terms of (mumbles) studies,

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:34.770
so some of the local Baltimore groups

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:36.350
have gotten involved in that

00:30:36.350 --> 00:30:38.830
with the African ancestry recruitment.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:40.870
I think Hafjazeera and Yasmin

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:43.300
are both on that advisory board.

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:45.470
I'm forgetting the name of the
principal investigators there

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:46.310
but I know they're working

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:48.440
with local church leaders
and things like that.

00:30:48.440 --> 00:30:49.520
Are there conversations

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:51.870
about trying to do similar
types of things like that

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:54.437
within NIA or at NIDA?

00:30:54.437 --> 00:30:55.630
I'm just curious,

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:57.240
I know we're outside of the
training realm a little bit,

00:30:57.240 --> 00:31:00.720
but that has such an
important impact on society

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:02.567
and what the trainees are walking into.

00:31:02.567 --> 00:31:04.988
I'm just curious to hear
how those conversations

00:31:04.988 --> 00:31:06.233
are going.
- Obviously it's connected.

00:31:06.233 --> 00:31:10.130
I think that one of the things
that is happening with NIA

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:15.130
that is now leading the
research on Alzheimer's disease

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:20.180
and related dementia is Jack
created a new initiative

00:31:21.480 --> 00:31:23.250
which his called CARD,

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:26.103
a Center for Alzheimer's
Disease and Related Dementia.

00:31:27.439 --> 00:31:31.270
We will have a new building in the test

00:31:31.270 --> 00:31:32.943
that is currently,

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:36.150
I will say half finished,

00:31:36.150 --> 00:31:37.543
we can see already.

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:40.490
But one of the thing that we want to do

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:42.653
is exactly what I said,

00:31:42.653 --> 00:31:46.210
do research on neurodegenerative disease,

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:49.510
they spa across multiple populations

00:31:49.510 --> 00:31:52.130
so we can leverage on the fact

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:54.110
that there are multiple condition

00:31:54.110 --> 00:31:56.160
and look at the differences

00:31:56.160 --> 00:31:58.380
and the similarity

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:00.870
and connect them to the knowledge.

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:04.313
I think that the genetic
assume much more sense

00:32:04.313 --> 00:32:08.920
and become much my informative
when it's diversified.

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:10.390
But one thing I want to say

00:32:10.390 --> 00:32:12.943
that is connected to what Amy said,

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:18.060
and that really is I struggle with

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:22.180
is that the Washington, Baltimore area

00:32:22.180 --> 00:32:25.397
is so filled with research institution,

00:32:25.397 --> 00:32:28.920
that people are kind of
tired in participating

00:32:30.100 --> 00:32:33.586
because we don't give them back enough.

00:32:33.586 --> 00:32:38.124
We need to find ways to
give back to the community.

00:32:38.124 --> 00:32:39.923
When we involve them,

00:32:39.923 --> 00:32:43.340
we need to find way that
people feel more involved,

00:32:43.340 --> 00:32:47.770
people feel that they're
part of a project,

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:52.770
they're not just our animal
model for our studies.

00:32:57.700 --> 00:33:01.670
And I do struggling to finding ways

00:33:01.670 --> 00:33:06.670
because even some of the
ethical constraints we live in

00:33:06.940 --> 00:33:10.133
not allow, for example,
to provide them care.

00:33:11.221 --> 00:33:14.090
But I think we need to
bend these boundaries.

00:33:14.090 --> 00:33:15.730
We need to find ways

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:19.010
so their research become
part of the community

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:23.220
and it's not using the
community to generate paper.

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:24.053
If we do that,

00:33:24.053 --> 00:33:26.660
I think that the involvement of people

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:28.580
will be much, much higher.

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:30.010
- Oh, that's so important.

00:33:30.010 --> 00:33:31.150
And it sounds, I mean you're hinting

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:34.360
at some of the policy
pieces around that as well,

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:38.574
but do you think there's also
a communication aspect of it?

00:33:38.574 --> 00:33:41.594
Or is it one or the other, both in parcel?

00:33:41.594 --> 00:33:43.603
- No, both of them, both of them.

00:33:43.603 --> 00:33:48.240
I think that when people come

00:33:48.240 --> 00:33:50.840
to the Baltimore
Longitudinal Study of Aging

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:55.440
and we ask them when they leave
after two, three day visit

00:33:55.440 --> 00:33:58.660
to write down their impression,

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:00.460
one of the thing they want to know

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:03.570
is what do you do with
the data that we give you.

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:05.400
And so they want newsletter,

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:06.820
they want to be informed,

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:10.230
they want to know what their contribution

00:34:10.230 --> 00:34:12.990
and their generosity resulting to.

00:34:12.990 --> 00:34:17.990
And we try to do something but
maybe it's not strong enough.

00:34:18.643 --> 00:34:19.790
- Hmm, yeah.

00:34:19.790 --> 00:34:21.190
I keep saying the same thing, but again,

00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:22.782
it sounds like community

00:34:22.782 --> 00:34:25.210
and that community that extends
outside of the research,

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:27.200
but is involved in the community at large

00:34:27.200 --> 00:34:30.410
and that we're all working
in the same goals together

00:34:30.410 --> 00:34:33.080
as a community, whether we're
participants or researchers.

00:34:33.080 --> 00:34:36.253
So I'll try not to keep saying
that, but it keeps coming up.

00:34:38.540 --> 00:34:39.373
- It's the answer.

00:34:39.373 --> 00:34:41.430
I think that that's a
brilliant idea, Luigi,

00:34:41.430 --> 00:34:44.540
on how to amplify what
we're giving back to them

00:34:44.540 --> 00:34:46.630
and help them recognize

00:34:46.630 --> 00:34:48.790
that they're getting
something out of it too,

00:34:48.790 --> 00:34:50.120
that's a really big challenge.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:52.780
We have a clinic that
we provide treatment,

00:34:52.780 --> 00:34:54.290
methadone and buprenorphine,

00:34:54.290 --> 00:34:56.800
for people who are addicted to opioids,

00:34:56.800 --> 00:34:59.940
but it's small and I think
we could expand that.

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:00.990
But then it gets into this

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:03.700
like are we using NIH research
dollars for treatment?

00:35:03.700 --> 00:35:07.260
And so, again, there's
these back and forth.

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:08.700
I don't know that there's a clean answer,

00:35:08.700 --> 00:35:10.530
but there's gotta be a better answer

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:12.263
than what we have right now.

00:35:14.230 --> 00:35:15.920
- And it seems so important that, I mean,

00:35:15.920 --> 00:35:17.570
in some ways the solution isn't there,

00:35:17.570 --> 00:35:18.790
but it's also encouraging to hear

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:21.290
that that's a point that
you're both wrestling with

00:35:21.290 --> 00:35:24.263
and thinking about and trying to improve.

00:35:24.263 --> 00:35:26.420
- You know, one thing that we
are really trying to increase

00:35:26.420 --> 00:35:28.580
is even like getting out
into the high schools

00:35:28.580 --> 00:35:30.910
and going to the science fairs

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:32.310
and being judges and being involved.

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:35.314
And maybe, again, if we
can get the kids involved

00:35:35.314 --> 00:35:36.750
and then they go home and
they talk to their parents

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:38.680
or their family members
or their church members

00:35:38.680 --> 00:35:40.550
and kind of, again, this,

00:35:40.550 --> 00:35:42.430
I'm sounding like I'm being redundant,

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:44.090
but this kind of grassroots way,

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:47.730
but I think that trickle
down just hasn't done it

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:50.910
and I think that maybe
that's another possibility.

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:53.920
- Yeah, yeah, so really
intentional in that sense as well.

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:57.160
- Yeah yeah, like we collect
money and we make little,

00:35:57.160 --> 00:36:00.220
we give money awards that come from us

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:01.870
for these Baltimore science fairs,

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:04.220
just it's not a lot but it's a little bit

00:36:04.220 --> 00:36:05.570
and not only do they win

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:08.500
but they get a little money
and it's kind of exciting.

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:09.860
I mean those are small things.

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:12.810
I think that there can be much
bigger ideas coming forward.

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:16.503
- Yeah, that's so
important in a lot of ways.

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:19.630
- I also want to loop
back and tie in, Amy,

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:21.250
one of the points that
you made earlier too

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:24.080
about the trainees and
everything they're doing

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:25.790
to really make large impact

00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:27.704
and the balance with the science.

00:36:27.704 --> 00:36:28.830
Because I feel like in a lot of ways,

00:36:28.830 --> 00:36:29.760
at least for me personally,

00:36:29.760 --> 00:36:31.330
I feel like the groups that are coming up

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:34.600
are doing a much better
job of that than I did

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:37.450
even when I was in that position.

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:39.540
You know, some of these
more public facing things

00:36:39.540 --> 00:36:41.300
I didn't start till I
was well into my career,

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:42.140
it's kind of settled,

00:36:42.140 --> 00:36:44.980
but I feel like that passion
is there and it's important

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:47.560
and they're running with
it in a lot of ways,

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:48.890
they're exemplary and things that,

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:50.500
we can all learn from that.

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:51.810
And then you also talked
about kind of the tension

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:54.330
between the research
and the outreach work.

00:36:54.330 --> 00:36:58.570
And so how are you all
encouraging that or modeling that?

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:01.611
And then also, similarly,
in your roles as well,

00:37:01.611 --> 00:37:03.370
are their ways that you
also try and balance that

00:37:03.370 --> 00:37:04.300
in your own lives?

00:37:04.300 --> 00:37:08.120
And how does that impact kind
