and how to balance that.

00:37:14.703 --> 00:37:16.902
So any insights that you all
have that you shared with

00:37:16.902 --> 00:37:18.152
or continue to?

00:37:19.907 --> 00:37:20.740
- I want to say it's true

00:37:20.740 --> 00:37:24.320
and we do Titanic effort.

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:28.480
I know that there's no one single weekend

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:30.230
that I don't try to work

00:37:30.230 --> 00:37:34.960
and to accomplish everything
that needs to be done.

00:37:34.960 --> 00:37:39.940
But to put a difficult note,

00:37:39.940 --> 00:37:43.430
I think that spending our day

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:46.560
from eight o'clock till
seven o'clock in the evening

00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:50.790
in front of Skype or Zoom

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:54.090
doesn't make our life easier.

00:37:54.090 --> 00:37:54.973
I think that,

00:37:56.120 --> 00:38:00.523
I feel like at the end of the
day, I'm completely exhausted.

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:06.470
So why doing the same thing
that I was doing before COVID-19

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:10.940
require double of the effort?

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:13.320
I think that's important
to understand this.

00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:15.140
And has we do it,

00:38:15.140 --> 00:38:16.710
that we are,

00:38:16.710 --> 00:38:20.150
let's say the struggling.

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:22.660
We need to be the one that never crack

00:38:22.660 --> 00:38:26.584
because people should see us as an anchor

00:38:26.584 --> 00:38:29.543
that they cannot be attached to.

00:38:30.400 --> 00:38:35.123
Our scientists, our staff
scientists, our trainees,

00:38:35.981 --> 00:38:38.110
are experiencing the same problem,

00:38:38.110 --> 00:38:40.980
the same kind of exhaustion.

00:38:40.980 --> 00:38:45.980
And so you go back to the
idea that the mutual support.

00:38:47.740 --> 00:38:49.810
I think that, and you're
right when you say

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:52.500
that this generation are,

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:54.010
especially in the trainee,

00:38:54.010 --> 00:38:58.070
people that are more
likely to help each other.

00:38:58.070 --> 00:39:00.250
Some of them get into crisis,

00:39:00.250 --> 00:39:04.570
the other come in and try
to be a support network.

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:08.310
This is certainly happening
in many of our labs,

00:39:08.310 --> 00:39:11.120
even when there is a real crisis.

00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:15.010
You can see that people get together,

00:39:15.010 --> 00:39:18.950
if not physically, at least virtually

00:39:18.950 --> 00:39:21.200
and create the support to each other.

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:23.360
And we need to encourage that.

00:39:23.360 --> 00:39:25.670
You know, for example, in my lab,

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:29.750
there's one of my staff
scientists organize happy hours

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:31.180
every Friday afternoon

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:35.062
and people come and go and they're there

00:39:35.062 --> 00:39:38.676
and that's an opportunity
for them to connect

00:39:38.676 --> 00:39:41.920
and also to hear what's
happening to others.

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:43.930
I say before,

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:46.850
my fear is that something is happening

00:39:46.850 --> 00:39:50.267
that will lead to something bad.

00:39:50.267 --> 00:39:54.320
And I don't perceive that
because I'm not there because,

00:39:54.320 --> 00:39:57.320
you know, you have
one-on-one conversation.

00:39:57.320 --> 00:40:02.080
The buzz that is in the building
that you can hear without

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:04.980
paying attention is no longer there.

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:07.400
And that is the part that we're missing

00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:09.403
that is really, really dangerous.

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:15.560
- I think that I have a very strong

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:17.193
self-preservation streak.

00:40:18.110 --> 00:40:21.717
So I know that I cannot work 24/7

00:40:21.717 --> 00:40:23.890
and in fact, I hate that term.

00:40:23.890 --> 00:40:25.810
One of the things that I do every week

00:40:25.810 --> 00:40:29.040
is I write a little message to
my whole intramural program,

00:40:29.040 --> 00:40:31.190
it's just like from my
heart to their ears,

00:40:32.125 --> 00:40:33.740
they can delete it or they can read it,

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:35.320
but it's always something personal.

00:40:35.320 --> 00:40:39.100
And one of my messages
some months back was 24/7,

00:40:39.100 --> 00:40:40.490
get rid of that term.

00:40:40.490 --> 00:40:43.060
Nobody really works 24/7

00:40:43.060 --> 00:40:45.810
and no one should even
say that they work 24/7.

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:46.660
And I think at the end,

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:47.760
I said something like,

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:51.260
the only thing you really
can do 24/7 is breathe

00:40:51.260 --> 00:40:53.790
and I support by that.

00:40:53.790 --> 00:40:56.650
- Yes, definitely well said, yeah,

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:58.242
so important.
- But I think that

00:40:58.242 --> 00:40:59.075
you have to model that.

00:40:59.075 --> 00:41:00.020
I think that it's just easy

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:03.170
to just always work and just
pedal as fast as you can

00:41:03.170 --> 00:41:07.560
and I do think that in my
messages and just in my life,

00:41:07.560 --> 00:41:11.600
I do try to exemplify that I love my work

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:13.147
and I love the work that we do

00:41:13.147 --> 00:41:15.120
and the collaborations
we have and so forth,

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:16.500
but you know what,

00:41:16.500 --> 00:41:20.220
I also made a dozen containers
of pesto this weekend

00:41:20.220 --> 00:41:22.240
because I harvested a lot of my basil

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:26.740
or I'm having 12 people for crabs tonight

00:41:26.740 --> 00:41:28.840
'cause my son just flew in from Wash-U,

00:41:28.840 --> 00:41:30.570
he's a neurology resident,

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:32.180
he just flew in this afternoon

00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:34.350
and we're having over all
of his friends for crabs.

00:41:34.350 --> 00:41:37.170
We have to show that we
also are human beings

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:39.860
and that we have to be able
to take care of yourself

00:41:39.860 --> 00:41:41.650
'cause if you're well is dry,

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:42.960
you don't want anything to give

00:41:42.960 --> 00:41:46.000
and I don't think that
that's good for anybody.

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:46.833
- That's really important.

00:41:46.833 --> 00:41:49.914
I'm glad that you're
being so vocal about that

00:41:49.914 --> 00:41:50.747
in a lot of ways too.

00:41:50.747 --> 00:41:52.020
Do you feel like that is

00:41:52.020 --> 00:41:53.830
getting incorporated into the culture?

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:55.780
Because I would say if I asked

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:57.240
many of the trainees that I work with,

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:59.250
they would say that's not
always their impression.

00:41:59.250 --> 00:42:01.550
Their impression is they
have to stay on the grind,

00:42:01.550 --> 00:42:03.680
this is their formative years.

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:04.870
If they take a step back

00:42:04.870 --> 00:42:06.410
or take a time to breathe and pause,

00:42:06.410 --> 00:42:08.030
someone else will continue that work

00:42:08.030 --> 00:42:09.750
and have the position
they were looking for.

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:12.210
So is that getting
incorporated in the culture

00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:16.183
or do you think this is a
unique perspective that you're?

00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:20.050
- What I can say about
that Nii is when I said,

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:21.535
we took a vacation this summer

00:42:21.535 --> 00:42:23.490
and one of my messages I was going on vaca

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:26.530
and I had so many people
send me a messages and say,

00:42:26.530 --> 00:42:30.330
thank you, now I feel
like I can go on vacation

00:42:30.330 --> 00:42:32.500
or I can tell people I'm going on vacation

00:42:32.500 --> 00:42:34.430
'cause we're so, in a leadership position,

00:42:34.430 --> 00:42:36.890
I think a lot of times
it was so afraid to admit

00:42:36.890 --> 00:42:38.400
that we've gone on vacation,

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:42.937
oh wow, we're not watching
the place every moment.

00:42:42.937 --> 00:42:46.350
And I think that it's that, but yeah,

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:48.792
people need to keep on working hard.

00:42:48.792 --> 00:42:51.560
- I think the balance
between life and work,

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:53.363
it's really important

00:42:53.363 --> 00:42:55.130
and of course we need to do,

00:42:55.130 --> 00:42:58.553
but I also think that
everybody's different.

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:03.720
I mean I don't think
that there is one measure

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:05.460
for everybody.

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:08.750
I think for some of the
trainees, for example,

00:43:08.750 --> 00:43:11.320
working really, really
hard give them the sense

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:15.380
that they're not losing the
opportunity of their career.

00:43:15.380 --> 00:43:19.230
Some are more relaxed
and they accept the idea

00:43:19.230 --> 00:43:23.000
that maybe that this is happening to them,

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:26.020
but I always reinforce the idea

00:43:26.020 --> 00:43:30.910
that NIH has been
fantastic in this crisis.

00:43:30.910 --> 00:43:32.470
We have given to people,

00:43:32.470 --> 00:43:34.910
we, they, I don't know who,

00:43:34.910 --> 00:43:38.293
we've given them incredible flexibility.

00:43:38.293 --> 00:43:41.460
You have to deal with the
children, deal with the children.

00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:43.180
You don't have to quit work.

00:43:43.180 --> 00:43:46.910
Because we understand that
the value of maintaining

00:43:46.910 --> 00:43:48.903
the health of our society,

00:43:50.710 --> 00:43:53.520
we alternate people, so we don't have

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:59.013
too much concentration of
people in the building.

00:44:00.928 --> 00:44:03.380
There has been incredible support.

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:06.733
I have to tell you that
this amount of flexibility,

00:44:07.700 --> 00:44:12.700
to some extent surprised
me that this was possible.

00:44:13.710 --> 00:44:15.970
It was really a discovery.

00:44:15.970 --> 00:44:19.560
And I think that has been
a really fantastic choice

00:44:20.448 --> 00:44:21.281
to do that

00:44:21.281 --> 00:44:24.750
because that allowed
people to relax enough

00:44:24.750 --> 00:44:26.670
in the moment of crisis.

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:28.440
But as I said,

00:44:28.440 --> 00:44:31.810
some people when I say tell them to relax,

00:44:31.810 --> 00:44:34.850
they just look at me like I'm crazy

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:36.920
because they don't want to relax.

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:41.920
They're losing ground so they
want to work night and day.

00:44:42.288 --> 00:44:43.740
And if they want to,

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:47.328
I can't really teach them who they are.

00:44:47.328 --> 00:44:48.597
They know who they are

00:44:48.597 --> 00:44:51.600
and so I think that I need to support them

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:53.253
for what they want to do.

00:44:54.178 --> 00:44:56.510
- Yeah, that's really
important, really well said.

00:44:56.510 --> 00:44:58.390
I love hearing about
that flexibility as well

00:44:58.390 --> 00:45:00.220
because it ties back to the point

00:45:00.220 --> 00:45:02.210
that you both made at
the beginning as well

00:45:02.210 --> 00:45:04.430
that things really seem to be continuing

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:06.770
in terms of the work and the productivity

00:45:06.770 --> 00:45:08.500
and that just shows me the flexibility,

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:10.920
even though some might
say that's detrimental

00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:11.890
from the way that you build it,

00:45:11.890 --> 00:45:13.760
it has not been detrimental at all

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:14.717
as things have continued

00:45:14.717 --> 00:45:17.870
and as people have been able
to continue to move forward.

00:45:17.870 --> 00:45:19.010
But to your point, Luigi, as well,

00:45:19.010 --> 00:45:22.150
just the individuality that's there

00:45:22.150 --> 00:45:25.930
and people have to know themselves
and what works for them.

00:45:25.930 --> 00:45:27.440
But Amy, it still sounds like in some ways

00:45:27.440 --> 00:45:28.950
you're also empowering people

00:45:28.950 --> 00:45:31.390
by just saying that you
are going on vacation

00:45:31.390 --> 00:45:32.937
and people are having the freedom

00:45:32.937 --> 00:45:34.720
to incorporate that as well.

00:45:34.720 --> 00:45:36.190
So it seems like on all layers,

00:45:36.190 --> 00:45:38.110
you're showing that

00:45:38.110 --> 00:45:40.640
but then also people are still
learning from it as well.

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:42.750
But I think it's such an important point

00:45:42.750 --> 00:45:46.150
to know that it's an
individually based piece.

00:45:46.150 --> 00:45:46.983
With new trainees that come in,

00:45:46.983 --> 00:45:49.493
I always tell them how I've had trainees

00:45:49.493 --> 00:45:51.660
that I've had to tell to
spend more time in the lab

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:53.000
because they weren't
putting the effort in,

00:45:53.000 --> 00:45:53.980
but I've also had trainees

00:45:53.980 --> 00:45:56.410
I've had to tell to spend
less time in the lab

00:45:56.410 --> 00:45:59.100
because they were spending
so much time overthinking,

00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:02.010
basically running themselves into a wall,

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:02.930
spreading themselves thin

00:46:02.930 --> 00:46:03.870
that when they took a pause,

00:46:03.870 --> 00:46:06.400
they were like, oh, I can
actually think more clearly

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:08.100
and use my time more efficiently

00:46:08.100 --> 00:46:10.660
and they actually got more
productive spending less time.

00:46:10.660 --> 00:46:12.110
That's obviously dangerous to share

00:46:12.110 --> 00:46:14.200
because sometimes people could be tempted

00:46:14.200 --> 00:46:15.890
to take that to extremes,

00:46:15.890 --> 00:46:17.390
but I think it's important to know

00:46:17.390 --> 00:46:19.870
that that balancing act is
there and people need to know

00:46:19.870 --> 00:46:21.060
where they need to take their breaks

00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:23.643
and where they need to
continue to move forward.

00:46:24.860 --> 00:46:27.300
- By the way, Amy, during the weekend

00:46:27.300 --> 00:46:31.883
I made tomato sauce and peach jam so.

00:46:33.852 --> 00:46:34.700
- I can't wait to come to your house

00:46:34.700 --> 00:46:36.033
and eat some of it, Luigi.

00:46:37.135 --> 00:46:37.968
- You can try them.

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:41.360
- Yes, there's definitely a
food vibe going on here as well.

00:46:41.360 --> 00:46:42.990
You both are gonna have to be careful

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:44.180
because you're gonna
have a lot of requests

00:46:44.180 --> 00:46:46.683
after this episode once people hear that.

00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:49.920
- One of the things that
Luigi did before the pandemic,

00:46:49.920 --> 00:46:51.508
and I'm sure he'll do it again,

00:46:51.508 --> 00:46:52.341
because we're really limited

00:46:52.341 --> 00:46:53.850
with what we can do with awards,

00:46:53.850 --> 00:46:56.468
so like poster awards
and things like that,

00:46:56.468 --> 00:46:58.760
you know, they get a little
paper plate, paper flap,

00:46:58.760 --> 00:47:02.340
and Luigi has the winners
to his house for dinner.

00:47:02.340 --> 00:47:04.030
I mean that's a really big sentiment.

00:47:04.030 --> 00:47:05.230
I love that and I think

00:47:05.230 --> 00:47:07.648
that I would love to
be able to do that too.

00:47:07.648 --> 00:47:09.010
Yeah, it's pretty cool.

00:47:09.010 --> 00:47:11.650
- That sounds like a
great incentive to me.

00:47:11.650 --> 00:47:12.483
- Trust me, if you've ever had-

00:47:12.483 --> 00:47:15.140
- Well come and visit and you know,

00:47:15.140 --> 00:47:16.590
some Italian food,

00:47:16.590 --> 00:47:18.613
you will not be hungry, I promise you.

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:21.750
- I'm excited already.

00:47:21.750 --> 00:47:24.490
Well I know that our time
has gone very quickly here,

00:47:24.490 --> 00:47:25.600
which doesn't surprise me

00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:26.640
and perhaps we'll have to do this again

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:28.136
in the future as well,

00:47:28.136 --> 00:47:29.844
but just to tie in

00:47:29.844 --> 00:47:32.323
and I'm just curious to hear
from both of you what you're,

00:47:33.170 --> 00:47:35.160
what you're hopeful for
in the months ahead.

00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:37.050
I mean you've both talked
about some of the resilience

00:47:37.050 --> 00:47:38.300
and the ways that we've been creative,

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:41.610
but what are you hoping
for as scientific directors

00:47:41.610 --> 00:47:44.140
and for your institutes and your trainees

00:47:44.140 --> 00:47:45.113
as we move forward?

00:47:46.610 --> 00:47:48.432
Luigi, we'll start with you this time.

00:47:48.432 --> 00:47:49.908
I know that's not a light question.

00:47:49.908 --> 00:47:54.840
- Well, you know, as I said,

00:47:54.840 --> 00:47:59.840
I hope that the grief of
COVID-19 will be a released soon

00:48:01.910 --> 00:48:05.990
and then we can take back our journal,

00:48:05.990 --> 00:48:08.570
but with the new awareness

00:48:09.432 --> 00:48:13.520
of what this pandemic taught us

00:48:13.520 --> 00:48:17.860
and how important it is to
remain connected as a community

00:48:19.100 --> 00:48:22.280
and how, as you said before,

00:48:22.280 --> 00:48:26.180
maintaining flexibility
will not put us down,

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:30.040
but it will actually
support us as a community

00:48:30.040 --> 00:48:32.140
and make us more productive.

00:48:32.140 --> 00:48:36.240
I think that my hope that
will come out from this

00:48:37.210 --> 00:48:41.260
fast enough so that we can fully recover.

00:48:41.260 --> 00:48:43.050
I have a little bit of fear too.

00:48:43.050 --> 00:48:48.050
And the fear is that if this
thing goes on for long time

00:48:48.370 --> 00:48:53.300
that process language,

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:55.593
that connection that we had,

00:48:56.450 --> 00:48:58.470
as more new people come in

00:48:58.470 --> 00:49:02.870
that will be lost and will
be very, very difficult

00:49:02.870 --> 00:49:04.119
to recreate,

00:49:04.119 --> 00:49:08.110
it will be a measured effort
to recreate that language

00:49:09.260 --> 00:49:13.010
that is informal and that is not verbal.

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:16.240
And I can certainly, I will certainly try

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:20.070
to push that project,

00:49:20.070 --> 00:49:24.320
but in the back of my mind,

00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:25.800
that's what I'm afraid of,

00:49:25.800 --> 00:49:30.253
although in general, I'm very positive

00:49:30.253 --> 00:49:31.770
attitude on this.

00:49:31.770 --> 00:49:33.332
- Right, I appreciate

00:49:33.332 --> 00:49:34.216
that honesty.
- And I want

00:49:34.216 --> 00:49:35.049
to embrace people.

00:49:35.049 --> 00:49:37.140
I want that.

00:49:37.140 --> 00:49:38.540
That's what I want.

00:49:38.540 --> 00:49:42.210
I want the world of
friendship and connection.

00:49:43.300 --> 00:49:45.080
- Yes, definitely hoping

00:49:45.080 --> 00:49:47.608
and looking forward to that as well.

00:49:47.608 --> 00:49:48.441
Amy, what about you?

00:49:48.441 --> 00:49:49.600
- I think that for me

00:49:49.600 --> 00:49:51.280
the thing that I'm most excited about

00:49:51.280 --> 00:49:54.740
is that during this time period
as I've just become the SD

00:49:54.740 --> 00:49:57.730
and under the under the
whole pandemic thing,

00:49:57.730 --> 00:50:00.770
we've been working pretty
hard behind the scenes

00:50:00.770 --> 00:50:03.290
to make some pretty big changes

00:50:03.290 --> 00:50:06.530
in the way that we do business at NIDA.

00:50:06.530 --> 00:50:09.850
And I am so excited to see this pan out.

00:50:09.850 --> 00:50:12.900
And I feel like we've
got the next year or two,

00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:14.940
there's a lot of new things
that we're bringing in.

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:17.640
and really I hope pushing the translation

00:50:17.640 --> 00:50:20.444
that I've always wanted to
have happen as a chemist.

00:50:20.444 --> 00:50:24.060
I'm really sure that
I've cured enough rats

00:50:24.060 --> 00:50:25.080
of cocaine abuse,

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:27.530
that I'd really like to
get that beyond rats.

00:50:27.530 --> 00:50:28.680
And I think that we've got,

00:50:28.680 --> 00:50:29.910
we're putting some things in place

00:50:29.910 --> 00:50:31.130
that I think are gonna be fantastic.

00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:32.330
We've got some new recruits

00:50:32.330 --> 00:50:34.510
that are not only fantastic themselves,

00:50:34.510 --> 00:50:36.480
but very committed to diversity,

00:50:36.480 --> 00:50:37.710
I think are gonna really bring in

00:50:37.710 --> 00:50:39.500
some new ideas for outreach

00:50:39.500 --> 00:50:42.310
and I'm just can't wait
for that to happen,

00:50:42.310 --> 00:50:44.640
but I recognize that it's not
going to happen right away.

00:50:44.640 --> 00:50:46.330
So I guess that what I'm hopeful for

00:50:46.330 --> 00:50:48.861
is that the lessons that we've learned

00:50:48.861 --> 00:50:49.694
and the resilience that we've demonstrated

00:50:49.694 --> 00:50:50.910
in the last 18 months

00:50:50.910 --> 00:50:52.910
will continue to follow us

00:50:52.910 --> 00:50:55.840
as we deal with whatever this coronavirus

00:50:55.840 --> 00:50:57.563
is going to deal to us.

00:50:58.445 --> 00:51:00.180
And I'm an optimist,

00:51:00.180 --> 00:51:01.840
we're going to get through this

00:51:01.840 --> 00:51:03.470
and we're going to be better for it.

00:51:03.470 --> 00:51:06.634
It's just, we just have to hang in there.

00:51:06.634 --> 00:51:08.544
- Mmm, mmm, so well said.

00:51:08.544 --> 00:51:10.587
Well I'm excited to hear about
the new initiatives as well

00:51:10.587 --> 00:51:12.130
and all that's been brewing

00:51:12.130 --> 00:51:14.220
and that you've all
been working on so hard.

00:51:14.220 --> 00:51:15.730
And I also appreciate

00:51:15.730 --> 00:51:18.788
I know just the sense
between you and Luigi

00:51:18.788 --> 00:51:21.020
and just the comradery
amongst the institutes

00:51:21.020 --> 00:51:23.440
and the similar aligning

00:51:23.440 --> 00:51:25.452
and parallel goals in a lot of ways.

00:51:25.452 --> 00:51:26.285
So it's encouraging for me to hear

00:51:26.285 --> 00:51:28.790
just the way that things have
continued to move forward.

00:51:28.790 --> 00:51:31.000
Definitely interested in
doing an extended conversation

00:51:31.000 --> 00:51:32.250
at some point as well

00:51:32.250 --> 00:51:34.710
and even coming back to some
of these ideas in the future.

00:51:34.710 --> 00:51:37.090
So hopefully at some point in person,

00:51:37.090 --> 00:51:39.430
even though we've all been
virtual up to this point,

00:51:39.430 --> 00:51:42.030
but definitely appreciate it.

00:51:42.030 --> 00:51:43.620
Go ahead, go ahead.

00:51:43.620 --> 00:51:44.980
- Anytime.

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:46.100
- Excellent.

00:51:46.100 --> 00:51:48.210
Well it's been great meeting
both of you officially.

00:51:48.210 --> 00:51:50.490
I know we've corresponded
beforehand and I appreciate

00:51:50.490 --> 00:51:52.370
again, just your level of candor

00:51:52.370 --> 00:51:54.912
that you shared on this Addy Hour podcast.

00:51:54.912 --> 00:51:57.360
I know this is gonna be a
unique setup in a lot of ways

00:51:57.360 --> 00:51:58.920
but it's something that I
think will really be important

00:51:58.920 --> 00:52:00.283
as we peel back the layers.

00:52:01.241 --> 00:52:03.790
So thank you again to Dr. Amy
Newman, Dr. Luigi Ferrucci

00:52:03.790 --> 00:52:05.370
for being willing to come

00:52:05.370 --> 00:52:07.290
and to join on this Addy Hour podcast.

00:52:07.290 --> 00:52:08.220
And to all our listeners,

00:52:08.220 --> 00:52:10.950
